          "question": "I have had three headaches this month, all on one side, with nausea and the light bothering me.",
          "answer": "Your description matches a pattern that the migraine summary describes: one-sided headaches with nausea and sensitivity to light (*Migraine*, General clinical reference). This is not a diagnosis. The same summary says not every attack has every feature, and that headaches can affect both sides. Only a clinician can work out what is causing yours.\n\n**Emergency check:** The safety check found no emergency signs in what you described. Get emergency care now if you have any of these:\n- A sudden headache that reaches full intensity within about a minute, or the worst headache of your life (*Sudden severe headache and other dangerous headache patterns*)\n- Fever with a stiff neck, or a rash that does not fade when pressed (*Signs suggesting meningitis*)\n- New confusion, weakness, numbness, trouble speaking, vision loss, or seizure (*Sudden severe headache*; *Sudden neurological symptoms that may indicate a stroke*)\n- Headache after a head injury, especially with vomiting or drowsiness\n\nThe safety check does not clear you. It only checks against a fixed list, so a symptom not on that list could still matter.\n\n**Next step:** Book an appointment with a doctor or nurse to talk about three headaches in one month. Before the visit, it helps to note when each headache started, how long it lasted, what you took for it, and how often you take pain relievers. The migraine guidance (*Migraine: acute treatment, when prevention starts, and medication-overuse limits*) notes that frequent use of acute headache medicine can keep headaches going, so mention how often you take them. Your clinician can decide on any treatment.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
